papers/64-lacunary-beta-entropy-approximants/PROOF_PACKAGE.md:9:- **Dependency map:** Parry 1960 lexicographic criterion → unique root of `F` → realization.
papers/64-lacunary-beta-entropy-approximants/PROOF_PACKAGE.md:18:- **Strategy:** repeat the suffix comparison on the terminated word; use monotonicity of root equations.
papers/64-lacunary-beta-entropy-approximants/PROOF_PACKAGE.md:20:- **Proof:** Every suffix of `a_1…a_{n_j}0^∞` is lexicographically smaller.  Its digit sum at `β_j` is one, so it is the greedy expansion.  Adding the `(j+1)`st term raises the partial sum at `β_j`, forcing `β_{j+1}>β_j`.  If the increasing roots had limit below `β`, a finite partial sum would already exceed one there, contradicting `S_j(β_j)=1` for large `j`.
papers/64-lacunary-beta-entropy-approximants/PROOF_PACKAGE.md:44:- **Proof:** `S_K(x)<F(x)≤S_K(x)+x^{-n_{K+1}}/(1−x^{-1})`.  Opposite signs at rational endpoints bracket `β`; finite sum signs bracket `β_j`; monotonicity brackets their ratio.
